Concepts and Definitions of Census Terms
This section lists and explains the main concepts and definitions of terms as they were used
in the collection of data during the 2008 Census.
Dwelling unit
Dwelling unit refers to a house or a flat that was designed and intended to be the living
quarter of one household.   A dwelling unit ca n be a detached and independent physical
structure or it can be a part of an apartment building.
Household
A household is a social unit consisting of one or more persons who sleep in the same
dwelling unit and have a common arrangement for economic life and consumption of food.
The persons who comprise a household are usua lly, but not always, re lated to one another
by kinship.  Most often, a household consists of a family unit plus one or more other
relatives.  A person who lives alone is also considered a household.
 Head of household
In regular households, the head of household is usually the person w ho is responsible for
all members, who makes the major decisions ab out family affairs and who is usually the
economic provider for the family.
Household member
A person who usually resides in  a household, whether registered  or not, is considered a
household member for census purposes. More specifically a household member is:
• A person who is registered as a member  of the household and living in that
household as the census reference time
• A person who is registered as a member  of the household but  living outside the
country
• A person who is temporarily registered a nd living in that household at the day of
census
• A person who is in the process of moving to another residence but whose registration
still remains with the original household;
• A person who is in the pr ocess of moving to another residence and whose
registration has already been deleted at the ri/up/gu/dong office but still staying in
the household at the time of the census.
• A child who was born before October 1 2008 but still not registered in the office
• A person who used to stay in the household but died after October 1 2008.

--- page 14 ----------------------------------------------------
2008 Census of Population of DPR Korea
8
Institutional or collective living quarters
Institutional or collective living quarters are structurally separate and independent places of
abode intended for habitation by large groups of individuals under one management.  They
include:
• Boarding houses, dormitories
• Homes for the aged, sanatoria, rehabilitation centers
• Orphanages, baby homes
• Penal institutions, military camps
Institutional population
Institutional population refers to  all the persons who live (or temporarily) in institutional
living quarters. However, officials and admi nistrative personnel of institutional living
quarters are not included in the institutiona l population since they are enumerated as
members of their regular households.
Nuclear household
A nuclear household is a type of househol d whose members comprise one and only one
basic family unit, that is, a married couple with or without unmarri ed children; or, one
parent and at least one unmarried child.  A nuclear household should not have a non-
relative as member.
Extended household
An extended household is a type of househol d whose members comprise one or more
nuclear families living together in one housing unit; or, one nuclear family with at least one
other relative of the househol d head.  Like a nuclear household, an extended household
should not have a nonrelative as member.
Class of Labor
Class of worker refers to one of the three cate gories into which the labor force is divided,
namely:  farmer, official and worker.  Farm ers refer to persons who work in state or
cooperative farms as agricultural worker.  Wo rkers are those who are employed in state
enterprises and state organs doi ng skilled or unskilled labour .  Officials refer to those
employed in state enterprises and state organs in senior positions engaging mainly in
mental work.

